29	/**
30	 * The claims on a sheet: listed newest first, added to a person and edited one at a time. A claim
31	 * added with no date takes the entry date while one is set, else the sheet's today (an edit with none, the sheet's today). Its due-by is fixed when it is added: the terms-th business day
32	 * after its date (skipping weekends and the sheet's holidays then), with its person's own terms, else the terms in force on that date of its person's grade then (else the
33	 * sheet's terms in force on it); it does not
34	 * move when its date or the terms change later. A claim is added as DRAFT and moves through its statuses
35	 * as {@link ClaimStatus} allows; a change it does not allow is refused. Opening a claim gives it
36	 * the sheet's next number (or, while the sheet numbers by period, the next within its period), which it
37	 * keeps when voided and is never given again. Only a DRAFT claim
38	 * can be edited; an OPEN one is changed by voiding it and adding a new one. Settling it
39	 * fixes its due at its figure (the due bound by a statement, else its person's grade rate of the
40	 * moment on the claim's date) with its late charge of the moment frozen in; with no figure it
41	 * stays live. A steward can re-settle a SETTLED claim at the figure it would have today as an open claim
42	 * bound by no statement, or void it within 7 days of its settlement: its frozen figure leaves every total
43	 * and it keeps its number. The user who opened a claim cannot settle it, and a clerk cannot settle one whose
44	 * due is above the sheet's clerk limit (a steward can). A claim with no proofs whose due is above the proof threshold
45	 * in force on its date cannot be settled or approved by anyone, and one with an empty note whose due is above the
46	 * note threshold in force on its date cannot be opened. No claim whose due-by falls inside a closed period can
47	 * be added, edited (to or from such a date), opened, settled, re-settled or voided. No claim can be added
48	 * or edited to a date after its person's retirement date. A claim with a negative amount is a
